+### syntax-error-006 — Real-time footage playback
+
+- Request: "the video player here is too laggy, I can't view uploaded video properly and run it"
+- Task type: Performance fix to one product owner, `src/app/engine/engine-footage.ts`, with its consumer `product-canvas.tsx`.
+- User-visible result: An uploaded video now plays smoothly in the preview while the timeline runs. Scrubbing still lands on the exact playhead frame, and MP4 export still renders exact frames.
+- Root cause: The preview seeked the hidden video element to the playhead on every timeline tick and awaited `seeked` before redrawing. A seek forces the browser to decode from the previous keyframe, so each animation frame cost a keyframe-to-target decode; on real footage that is tens to hundreds of milliseconds per frame, which is the lag.
+- Decision: The element now decodes in real time. While `timeline.isPlaying` is true and no viewport gesture holds the frame, `playFootage` starts the looping element and only re-syncs `currentTime` when it drifts more than 250 ms from the playhead; `onFootageFrame` polls `currentTime` on animation frames and re-samples the sheet once per new video frame. When paused or scrubbing, `seekFootage` parks the element on the exact frame as before, but concurrent requests coalesce to the newest target so a scrub never queues intermediate decodes. An exact seek holds live playback for 250 ms so export, which seeks frame after frame, is never restarted between artifact frames; playback resumes on the next playing tick once seeks stop. The sampling `OffscreenCanvas` is shared across rasterizations instead of allocated per frame.
+- Alternatives rejected: `requestVideoFrameCallback` — rejected because the element is never in the document and Chromium only reports composited frames for it; the callback never fired. Attaching the element to the page — rejected because product code may not mount media into the runtime host. Moving decode to WebCodecs — out of scope; the runtime reserves its codec library for export.
+- Performance intent: ordinary-product-work
+- Verification: `npx tsc -p tsconfig.json --noEmit` passes; `engine-motion`, `engine-camera`, `media-upload` and `video-export-settings` tests pass, 54 of 54. In headless Chromium with a 4 s VP9 test clip on the 1080×1080 artboard, six preview screenshots taken 300 ms apart were all distinct after the change against three of six before, and the hidden element advanced in real time with `paused: false` and no pending seek. Export MP4 through the sticky action produced a 4 s 1080×1080 artifact whose frames 10 and 70 show different footage, and the preview resumed animating after the export finished. `check-toolcraft-code-health.mjs` reports only the pre-existing `engine-footage.ts` boundary violations recorded in syntax-error-005.
+- Risks: Risk: The preview re-samples the source at the clip's frame rate; a 4K clip at the finest cell pitch still spends several milliseconds per frame in the summed-area pass. Risk: Live playback needs the browser's autoplay policy to allow a muted element to play; when it refuses, the preview falls back to the parked frame.
